        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. How We Chose These Bikes</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these bikes. They were chosen from manufacturer product pages and
            specification tables, warranty terms, recall records and independent hands-on coverage, and
            the claims on this page are limited to what those sources support. The site's
            <a href="/editorial-standards/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">editorial standards</a>
            explain the process in full.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What we looked for.</strong> We started from the access and comfort features that
            matter most to older riders and worked back to bikes: a step-through frame described as such
            by the maker and by an independent source; a seat with a backrest, or for the upright and
            the pedal exerciser a clear reason to go without; a console that is either simple or easy to
            read; resistance that starts low and changes in small steps; a weight rating agreed between
            sources; and a maker that offers delivery, assembly or both. Every bike also had to be on
            sale from its maker or a major retailer on 19 October 2026.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Consistency with our other guides.</strong> Four of these bikes also appear in our
            <a href="/best-recumbent-bikes/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">recumbent bike roundup</a>,
            checked the same day, and their figures here are the same. The DeskCycle 2 figures match our
            <a href="/best-under-desk-ellipticals/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">under-desk ellipticals and bikes</a>
            guide.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Recalls.</strong> We searched the CPSC recall database for Schwinn, Horizon and its
            parent Johnson Health Tech, SOLE, Marcy and its maker Impex, Sunny Health &amp; Fitness,
            DeskCycle, Peloton, BowFlex and Nautilus on 19 October 2026. We found no recall of any bike
            on this page. The older recalls we did find were for other products: Horizon treadmills in
            2022, Schwinn-branded ellipticals sold by Nautilus in 2011, BowFlex adjustable dumbbells in
            2025 and Impex dumbbell handles in 2021. If you are buying used, search the exact model
            number on cpsc.gov first.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What we left out, and why.</strong> The original Peloton Bike+ (model PL02) was
            recalled in November 2025 over a seat post that can break, after Peloton's 2023 recall of
            about 2.2 million original Bikes (PL01) for the same kind of failure; neither belongs on a
            list for riders for whom a fall is a serious matter, and the recalled units are still sold
            second-hand. We also left out indoor cycling bikes, whose high saddles and forward-leaning
            position are the wrong starting point for most older riders; budget folding "X-bikes", whose
            light frames and small seats suit occasional use rather than daily riding by someone who
            needs stability; and the Schwinn 130 upright, a good-value bike whose console Garage Gym
            Reviews notes is not backlit and whose frame Schwinn does not describe as step-through. Air
            bikes are excluded too: their resistance rises steeply with effort, which is the opposite of
            a gentle start.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-type" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Recumbent, Upright or Seated Pedal Exerciser: Which Suits You</h2>
<p>
            "Exercise bike" and "stationary bike" cover three quite different machines for an older
            rider. Choose the type first; the model matters much less.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Recumbent bikes</strong> put you in a reclined seat with a backrest and the pedals in
            front. A step-through recumbent is the most accessible bike you can buy: you walk into the gap
            beside the seat, sit down as you would on a chair, and lift your feet onto the pedals. The
            backrest supports your trunk, so you are not balancing on a saddle, and a fall while riding is
            very unlikely. The costs are length — the recumbents here run from about 52 to 66 inches —
            and a slightly gentler workout for the hips, because the leg angle differs from walking. For
            most people over 65 who want regular cardio and are unsure about balance, this is the type to
            start with.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Step-through uprights</strong> keep the familiar sit-up position of a bicycle, with
            the pedals beneath you, but lower the frame so you do not need to swing a leg over a high bar.
            They are shorter than recumbents, often by 20 inches, and the pedaling position is closer to
            outdoor cycling. You sit on a saddle without a backrest, though, so they suit riders who are
            steady on their feet and comfortable on a bicycle seat. The Horizon 5.0U is the example on
            this page. Some people with back pain find the upright position harder; others find it
            easier than reclining. Try both if you can.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Seated pedal exercisers</strong> are small pedal units you put on the floor in front
            of your own chair or under a desk. There is nothing to get on at all, which makes them the
            right answer for anyone for whom climbing onto any machine is a risk, and for keeping legs
            moving during long periods of sitting. They are light work rather than a cardio session, the
            unit can slide unless it is stabilized, and you need a sturdy chair without wheels. Our
            <a href="/best-under-desk-ellipticals/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">under-desk ellipticals and bikes</a>
            guide covers them in detail; the DeskCycle 2 is the one we list here.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>And two alternatives.</strong> If you want your arms working too while seated, a
            recumbent cross trainer — a seated stepper with moving handles — is often a better fit than
            a bike; see our guide to the
            <a href="/best-recumbent-ellipticals/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best recumbent ellipticals</a>.
            And if you want something weight-bearing, which a bike is not, the
            <a href="/best-ellipticals-for-seniors/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best ellipticals for seniors</a>
            and
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-seniors/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills for seniors</a>
            guides cover standing machines chosen on the same safety terms.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="getting-on" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Step-Through Height, Seat Height and Handles</h2>
<p>
            Getting on and off is where most falls around exercise bikes happen, and it is the feature
            listings describe least precisely. Almost no maker publishes a step-over height, so use the
            evidence you can get.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Step-over.</strong> "Step-through" only means there is no high bar to swing a leg
            over. How low and how wide the gap is still varies. OutdoorGearLab, which reviewed both,
            found the Marcy ME-709's step-through lower and more open than the Schwinn 290's, which has a
            narrower channel for your foot. Side-on photographs and independent reviews are the best guide;
            if you can, stand beside the bike in a store and step in once before buying.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Seat height.</strong> A seat close to the height of a dining chair is the easiest to
            sit on and rise from. Sunny publishes a seat height of 22.5 to 24.2 inches for its
            SF-RB422003, which is in that range; few other makers give the figure. If rising from low
            chairs is hard for you, ask the seller for the seat height at its lowest and highest settings,
            or measure in a store.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Handles.</strong> Fixed handles beside the seat give you something to hold as you
            lower yourself and stand up, and should not flex when you lean on them. Horizon lists
            handlebars at the console and beside the seat on the 5.0R, Schwinn lists stationary handlebars
            for getting on and off on the 290, and the Marcy has contoured, padded handlebars beside the
            seat. Moving handles are a different thing: on a bike with moving arms, or a cross trainer,
            never use them to steady yourself while mounting.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Pedals and straps.</strong> Weighted pedals hang the right way up, so you do not have
            to flip them with a foot. The Schwinn 290 and Marcy ME-709 have weighted pedals with
            straps; keep straps snug enough that a foot cannot slide off but loose enough to
            remove quickly.
          </p>
          <p>
            A simple routine makes any bike safer to mount. Put it on a level, non-slip floor — a firm
            <a href="/best-treadmill-mats/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">equipment mat</a> helps on
            hard floors — and leave clear space on the side you get on. Stand beside the seat, hold the
            fixed handle, sit fully, then lift one foot at a time onto the pedals. Reverse it to get off,
            and wait until the pedals have stopped. Our
            <a href="/best-recumbent-bikes/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">recumbent bike guide</a>
            covers seat travel and back support in more depth.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="console-resistance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Readable Consoles and Low Starting Resistance</h2>
<p>
            Two features rarely make the headline of a bike listing and matter a great deal to an older
            rider: whether you can read and operate the console, and how easy the easiest setting is.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Consoles.</strong> Bigger is not always better. A large touchscreen is easy to read
            but can bury the controls you need under menus, while a plain display with a few labelled
            buttons can be simpler to use. Look for high contrast and a backlight. Horizon describes the
            5.0R and 5.0U displays as high-contrast LCDs with a 60 by 127 mm window; the SOLE LCR has a
            10.1-inch touchscreen whose menus Garage Gym Reviews found shallow and easy to navigate;
            Schwinn's 290 has a 7-inch LCD. At the simple end, the Marcy shows time, speed, distance and
            calories on a basic LCD with a resistance knob, which some people find easier than buttons.
            Check, too, whether the bike needs an app to do anything useful. None of these does: every
            bike here can be started with a single button or by simply pedaling.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Low starting resistance.</strong> Makers publish the number of levels, not how light
            the lightest one is, so the resistance count is only a rough guide. What it does tell you is
            the size of each step. A bike with 100 levels, like the Horizon 5.0R and 5.0U, lets you add
            effort in very small increments, which suits anyone building up slowly; a bike with eight
            levels, like the Marcy, jumps further between settings. All the bikes here use magnetic or
            electronically controlled magnetic resistance, which is quiet, smooth and adjustable without
            stopping. We would not choose a friction-pad bike for a beginner of any age, because the
            resistance knob is harder to set consistently.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Programs and heart rate.</strong> Built-in programs are optional extras. A manual
            mode, where you choose a level and pedal, is what most older riders use most. If your doctor
            has given you a heart-rate range, a chest strap is more reliable than handle grips; several of
            these bikes accept Bluetooth straps, and our
            <a href="/best-heart-rate-monitors-for-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">heart-rate monitor guide</a>
            covers straps that work with bikes too. If you take a beta blocker or similar medication,
            heart-rate targets may not apply to you at all; ask your doctor how to judge effort instead.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="capacity-assembly" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Weight Capacity, Delivery and Assembly Help</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Weight capacity.</strong> The maximum user weight is a structural rating, and a
            margin above your own weight usually means a steadier frame. The bikes here are rated from
            220 lb (the compact Sunny) to 350 lb (the SOLE LCR); the Schwinn 290 is rated for 330 lb and
            the Horizons and Marcy for 300 lb. If you are close to a bike's rating, choose the next one up.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Delivery.</strong> Most of these bikes arrive in a large box on a parcel or freight
            service, usually to the front door or curb. Schwinn's and SOLE's sites both offer paid
            upgrades: on 19 October SOLE listed curbside delivery for the LCR at $49.99, room-of-choice
            delivery for $200 more and room-of-choice delivery with assembly for $350 more, figures Garage
            Gym Reviews also gives, and Schwinn listed a $299 White Glove option, with delivery to a room
            of choice and full assembly, on the bike pages we checked. Delivery terms change, so check the
            current options at checkout.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Assembly.</strong> Even a "mostly assembled" bike usually needs the seat, handlebars,
            pedals and console fitted. Garage Gym Reviews says the SOLE LCR arrives mostly assembled and
            should take under 30 minutes, but heavier bikes are a two-person job to unpack and position:
            the LCR weighs 152 lb, the Schwinn 290 about 108 lb and the Horizon 5.0R 123 lb by their
            makers' figures. If you live alone, are recovering from surgery or simply do not want to lift
            and kneel for an hour, pay for assembly or ask a family member to do it, and do not ride until
            every bolt has been checked once more after the first week.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Returns and funding.</strong> Schwinn's and Horizon's sites both described a 30-day
            return window on 19 October, with shipping deducted on Schwinn's; SOLE listed a 30-day
            satisfaction guarantee. Check terms before you buy, because returning a 100 lb bike is a
            nuisance. SOLE says the LCR is HSA/FSA eligible; whether you can use those funds depends on
            your plan, and our page on whether
            <a href="/are-treadmills-hsa-fsa-eligible/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">fitness machines are HSA/FSA eligible</a>
            explains how that usually works.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="starting-safely" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Starting Safely: Your Doctor, Balance and Effort</h2>
<p>
            This is an equipment guide, so this section is about using the machine sensibly, not about
            what exercise is right for your body. That is a conversation for your doctor or
            physiotherapist, and it is worth having before you buy if any of the conditions in the note
            at the top of this page applies to you.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Why bikes suit many older riders.</strong> Pedaling is non-weight-bearing and has no
            impact, the seat takes the work of balancing away, and on a recumbent the backrest supports
            your trunk. For people who feel unsteady on a treadmill or outdoors, that combination makes
            regular cardio realistic.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What a bike does not do.</strong> It provides little of the weight-bearing load that
            helps maintain bone density, and it does not train balance or walking. Many people combine a
            bike with walking and some strength work for that reason, and a physiotherapist can suggest
            balance exercises that suit you. If balance is the problem you are trying to solve, a bike is
            the safe way to do cardio while you work on it, not the cure.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Effort.</strong> Start at the lowest level for a few minutes, at a pace at which you
            could hold a conversation, and build the time before you build the resistance. Stop and get
            advice if you feel chest pain or pressure, unusual breathlessness, dizziness or
            light-headedness, or pain in a joint that does not settle. Stand up slowly at the end, holding
            the fixed handle, because some people feel light-headed when they rise after exercise.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Implanted devices.</strong> SOLE warns on its product pages that some of its machines
            include wireless charging, which emits a magnetic field that may interfere with implanted
            medical devices, and that anyone with such a device should consult their doctor before use.
            The LCR lists wireless charging. If you have a pacemaker or another implant, ask your doctor
            or the device maker about any machine with wireless charging or strong magnets.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Joint replacements.</strong> If you have had a hip or knee replaced, ask your surgeon
            or physiotherapist when you can start and how far forward the seat may go, because some
            post-operative precautions limit how far the hip should bend. Our page on whether
            <a href="/are-treadmills-bad-for-your-knees/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills are bad for your knees</a>
            and the
            <a href="/treadmill-vs-exercise-bike/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill vs exercise bike</a>
            comparison cover the wider trade-offs between walking and cycling.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best exercise bike for seniors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most older riders, a step-through recumbent such as the Schwinn 290: a seat with lumbar support, side handles for getting on and off, a 330 lb rating and simple controls. The Horizon 5.0R is a cheaper alternative with very fine resistance steps, and the Horizon 5.0U suits confident riders who prefer to sit upright.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a recumbent or upright bike better for seniors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A recumbent is usually better: the backrest supports your trunk, the seat is close to chair height, and a step-through frame makes it easy to get on and off. An upright suits riders who are steady on a bicycle seat, want a shorter machine or find reclining uncomfortable. If you choose an upright, pick a step-through frame.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best stationary bike for seniors with balance problems?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A step-through recumbent with fixed handles beside the seat, or a seated pedal exerciser used from a sturdy chair, because neither asks you to balance on a saddle. Set it up on a level, non-slip floor with clear space on the side you get on. Ask your doctor or physiotherapist first if you have dizziness or a balance disorder.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much weight should an exercise bike hold for an older rider?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Choose a bike rated comfortably above your weight, ideally by 50 lb or more, because a higher rating usually means a steadier frame. The bikes on this page range from 220 lb for the compact Sunny to 350 lb for the SOLE LCR, with most at 300 to 330 lb.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are pedal exercisers good for seniors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They are a good option for people who should not climb onto any machine, or who want to keep their legs moving while seated. They give lighter exercise than a full bike. Use one from a heavy chair without wheels, on a non-slip surface, so neither the chair nor the pedals can slide.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can someone else assemble an exercise bike for me?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Several makers sell delivery with assembly: on 19 October 2026 SOLE listed room-of-choice delivery with assembly for the LCR at $350 extra, and Schwinn listed a $299 White Glove option on the bike pages we checked. Terms change, so check at checkout. Otherwise, ask a family member or a local handyman service.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying an Exercise Bike for a Senior</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Each of these comes up often when a bike is bought for an older rider, often by a well-meaning relative.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a spin bike because it is the best-reviewed</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Indoor cycling bikes top most rankings, and they are the wrong machine for most older riders: a high saddle, a forward lean and no backrest. Start from how the person will get on and sit, then look at reviews. For most, that means a step-through recumbent.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing on the screen instead of the console controls</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A large touchscreen looks easy and can hide basic controls behind menus, logins and app prompts. Check that the bike can be started with one button or simply by pedaling, that the display has high contrast or a backlight, and that resistance can be changed without looking for it.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Starting at a level that feels like exercise</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Many new riders pick a resistance that feels like a workout on day one and are sore or put off by day three. Start at the lowest level for a few minutes at a conversational pace, build the time over weeks, and only then add resistance in small steps.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving assembly to the person who will ride it</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Unboxing and building a 100 lb bike means lifting, kneeling and tightening bolts for an hour or more. For an older buyer, pay for assembly or arrange help, and recheck the bolts, pedals and seat lock after the first week of use.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

